{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788139000,"nonce":"Wx6Cz+HXxxjeMyJOz0534WvXa+Y=","message":"OPEN-QUESTION RESOLUTIONS — 2.4 worker, branch `spec/2.4-world-serializers`.\nAll three resolved inside this spec; none of them changes another spec's contract.\n\n\n**Resolved: the taxonomy is the game's, not ours. Twelve of fourteen words map\nto a `ThingRequestGroup`; the other two name a `ThingDef` property.**\n\n    food      -\u003e group FoodSourceNotPlantOrTree   (the word 2.3's `nearest` already uses)\n    meds      -\u003e group Medicine                   (ditto)\n    apparel   -\u003e group Apparel                    (ditto)\n    weapons   -\u003e group Weapon                     (ditto)\n    drugs     -\u003e group Drug\n    corpses   -\u003e group Corpse\n    chunks    -\u003e group Chunk\n    art       -\u003e group Art\n    plants    -\u003e group Plant\n    beds      -\u003e group Bed\n    buildings -\u003e group BuildingArtificial\n    haulable  -\u003e group HaulableEver               (the default when nothing is asked)\n    all       -\u003e group Everything\n    resources -\u003e group HaulableEver + ThingDef.CountAsResource\n\nRationale, three parts:\n\n- **A group is a membership list the game already maintains.** `ListerThings`\n  keeps one list per group and fills it in `Add()` from\n  `ThingListGroupHelper.Includes`. Reading one is a list read; inventing a\n  bucket would be a predicate over every thing on the map, and it would drift\n  from the game's own answer the first time a DLC or a bench mod adds a def.\n- **The four words 2.3 already shipped keep their meaning.** `nearest\n  --category food|meds|apparel|weapons` resolves to exactly these four groups.\n  Two verbs answering \"food\" differently would be the worse defect.\n- **`resources` has no group, so it borrows the resource readout's own\n  definition.** `ThingDef.CountAsResource` is `resourceReadoutPriority !=\n  Uncounted` — literally what the top-left readout counts, which is what a\n  player means by \"resources\".\n\nEvery result publishes `query.category_source` (e.g. `\"group:Apparel\"`,\n`\"group:HaulableEver + ThingDef.CountAsResource\"`), so a consumer reads the\ndefinition instead of guessing it, and a future word cannot quietly redefine an\nold one.\n\n\n**Resolved: a bounded category-tree walk over the real `allowedDefs` set, with\nthe denominator always named. Neither of the two obvious routes may be used,\nand both for reasons worth recording.**\n\n- `ThingFilter.Summary` is read-only and **useless**. Its first two branches\n  need the XML-only `thingDefs` / `categories` lists, which a runtime storage\n  filter does not have, and its third needs exactly one allowed def. A real\n  stockpile filter falls all the way through to the literal\n  `\"UsableIngredients\"` translation — the same four words for every stockpile\n  on the map.\n- `ThingFilter.DisplayRootCategory` — the field the storage tab actually draws\n  from — is a **lazy-init getter that runs a `GenThreading.ParallelFor` over\n  every ThingCategory node crossed with every allowed def**, and whose setter\n  then runs `RecalculateSpecialFilterConfigurability()`, writing\n  `allowedHitPointsConfigurable` and `allowedQualitiesConfigurable`. An\n  observer must not be the thing that runs it.\n\nSo `FilterSummary.Build(filter, universe, universeName)`:\n\n- one bottom-up pass credits each def's categories to the root, once per node\n  per def (a def in two categories sharing an ancestor is not double-counted),\n  building `allowed` from `filter.AllowedThingDefs` (the real HashSet) and\n  `total` from the universe;\n- the walk then descends from `ThingCategoryDefOf.Root` **only into nodes that\n  are partially allowed**. A node that is all-in or all-out is one line and its\n  whole subtree is dropped — that is the compression. Bounded by\n  `NodeCap = 24`, `DepthCap = 4`, with `categories_more` publishing what the cap\n  cost;\n- a filter with \u003c= 12 allowed defs also gets `defs: [...]` verbatim, because\n  \"steel, plasteel\" beats \"ResourcesRaw 2/38\";\n- `hp_range_pct` and `quality_range` come from the plain field getters and\n  appear only when they are not the full range.\n\n**The denominator is always named in `universe`**, because \"8/12\" is\nuninterpretable without it: a stockpile passes its own\n`GetParentStoreSettings().filter` (the EverStorable fixed filter — exactly what\nits tab could offer), a bill passes `recipe.fixedIngredientFilter`, and an\noutfit/food policy has no natural parent so it falls back to every ThingDef in\nthe category (`universe:\"all-thingdefs\"`, computed once per session).\n\n\n**Resolved: a three-tier ladder under ONE window vocabulary — 1.7's — with the\nopaque tier scraping FIELDS ONLY, never properties.**\n\nThe head of every window entry is 1.7's four fields verbatim\n(`type`, `type_full`, `title?`, `layer`) plus `force_pause` and\n`absorbs_input`; `kind` says which tier answered. The force-pause half of the\nresult is `TimeDriver.ForcePausePayload(stack)`'s output unmodified under\n`force_pause`, so rwtest asserts on one shape.\n\n1. **`node-tree`** — `Dialog_NodeTree` and every subclass, which is what EVERY\n   ChoiceLetter opens (`ChoiceLetter.OpenLetter` builds a DiaNode from\n   `Choices` and stacks a `Dialog_NodeTreeWithFactionInfo`). `options[]` is read\n   off the current node: `{index, label, disabled, disabled_reason, closes,\n   opens_node}`.\n2. **`message-box`** — `Dialog_MessageBox`, three public button-text fields.\n   Mods reach for it constantly (the charity confirmation inside\n   `ChoiceLetter_AcceptVisitors` is vanilla doing exactly that).\n3. **`opaque`** — everything else. The window is scraped for candidate text:\n   instance fields only, walking the type chain up to `Window`, collecting\n   strings, TaggedStrings and the labels of any `List\u003cDiaOption\u003e` or\n   `List\u003cFloatMenuOption\u003e` it holds. Capped at 8 values, 160 chars each.\n\n**Fields, never properties, and that is the load-bearing part of the\nresolution.** A field read cannot execute game code. A property getter on a\nwindow class we have never seen is arbitrary code, and this project has now\nfound lazy-init getters that write the save in `Pawn_OutfitTracker`,\n`Zone_Growing`, `ResearchManager` and `Bill_Production` — reflecting over\nproperties on Hospitality's, Gastronomy's, Storefront's and Guests' windows\nwould be inviting that class of bug from a mod we cannot audit. The restriction\nis what makes tier 3 safe to run sight unseen, which is what the session-4\namendment asked for.\n\nNothing in `interactions` closes, focuses, reorders or answers a window.\nDeciding what a dialog MEANS stays 3.5's.\n\n\n**ChoiceLetter option labels need reflection.** `DiaOption.text` is\n`protected string` with no public accessor at all (`Verse/DiaOption.cs`), so\nthe words on the button come from an `AccessTools` field ref, bound once and\ntolerantly, with `source: \"backing-field\" | \"unavailable\"` published beside the\ndata — PawnSafe's discipline. `interactions` reports `label: null` with\n`source:\"unavailable\"` rather than an empty string, so \"we could not look\" and\n\"the button has no text\" stay distinguishable.","files":null}]}